WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.060
You have been building a transcription workflow for research recordings.

2
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.940
Thank you for joining us today, Dr Rossi.

3
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.460
What problem were you trying to solve?

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.920
Research interviews are full of personal information: names, places and details that could identify a participant.

5
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:22.760
Uploading them to a cloud service moves that information outside the institution, which many ethics approvals do not allow.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.600
This workflow runs every step on hardware the institution controls, whether that is a laptop or a university cluster.

7
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.460
Which models does it rely on?

8
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.720
Speech is transcribed by Whisper through the Hugging Face transformers library, and speakers are told apart by a pyannote diarisation pipeline.

9
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.120
The model weights are downloaded once, and after that a run needs no network connection.

10
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.780
What happens to the text once the model has produced it?

11
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:59.400
A series of clean-up stages removes stray characters, repairs spacing and punctuation, and can shorten the runaway repetitions that Whisper occasionally produces.

12
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.160
Personal names are then replaced with placeholders, using a curated dictionary of first names and surnames.

13
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.020
Does the masking find every name?

14
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.240
No, and it matters to say so.

15
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:19.520
A dictionary finds only the names it contains, and only when they are capitalised, so an unusual or lower-case name can slip through.

16
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.280
Every masked transcript therefore recommends a review, and the paper reports how often names were missed.

17
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.220
How far can a researcher trust the speaker labels?

18
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.720
That depends on the recording.

19
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:40.360
Overlapping speech, similar voices and distant microphones all make attribution harder, so the labels should be checked against the audio wherever exact attribution matters.

20
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.320
The paper reports the error rates we measured on meeting recordings.

21
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.180
What do the timestamped files contain?

22
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:55.080
The JSON file keeps the timing of every segment and every word, and the caption file uses the same timings.

23
00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:05.160
Both are written before any clean-up, so they record exactly what the model produced, and with masking switched on a redacted copy of each is written beside them.

24
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.120
A review file then points to segments worth checking by ear.

25
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.060
And how would someone check a transcript months later?

26
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:22.400
Each run writes a manifest that lists every file it produced with a digest of its contents, together with the settings and software versions.

27
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.720
Checking the files against that manifest shows whether anything has changed since.

28
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.500
Thank you, Elena.

29
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.940
That was very clear.

